/* ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
   ZeroStick EXO — EDITORIAL CLARITY.

   LOAD ORDER (this matters, it is the whole contract):
       phone.html <style> #1     line 7    the kit + the palette
       phone.html <style> #2     line 587  entrances + the five editor hues
       zs-brand.css              line 606  materials, press, the ion accent
       phone.html <style> #3     line 607  reduced-motion entrances
       zs-anim.css               line 2434 ─ IN THE BODY
       zs-micro.css              line 2440 ─ IN THE BODY
       zs-editorial.css   ← THIS FILE, linked at line ~2446 (LIVE since
                            2026-08-14), immediately after zs-micro.js

   THE <link> IS IN THE BODY, AFTER zs-micro.css — never move it to <head>.
   Two of this app's stylesheets load from the body, so a <head> link is NOT
   last and several rules here would silently lose. The shipped order is:

       <link rel="stylesheet" href="zs-micro.css">
       <script src="zs-micro.js"></script>
       <link rel="stylesheet" href="zs-editorial.css">

   Rules that must also beat the two stylesheets JavaScript injects at RUNTIME
   (smart-actions.js `zs-smart-css`, phone-sections.js `cv-region-css`, both
   appended to <head> after everything) cannot win on order at all, so they
   are prefixed with `body` and win on specificity. Marked where they appear.

   THREE THINGS THIS FILE DELIBERATELY DOES NOT TOUCH, because they are owned
   elsewhere and stomping them would break shipped behaviour:
     · `.sw::before`   zs-micro.css's ion bloom  — see §9.1
     · `.pk::after`    zs-micro.css's lock ring  — see §10
     · `.item .ico.ico-*`  the five editor hues  — see §5.3

   WHAT THIS FILE CHANGES, AND WHY

   The kit had nine box treatments separated by 2px of corner radius, four
   "pick one of N" controls with four geometries, sixty padding values, and a
   type range 6.5px wide from 12px to 18px. The section header was BOLDER than
   the row title it introduced. Everything looked like one thing because,
   measured, everything WAS one thing.

   Three moves fix it, in this order of importance:

   1. TYPE DOES THE WORK. Seven sizes, three weights, size-specific tracking.
      A config name is 24px/700; the label above it is 11.5px/700 uppercase
      muted. That is a 2.1× size ratio, and the hierarchy stops being inverted.

   2. SURFACES GET A JOB, NOT A RADIUS. There are now exactly four materials —
      GROUND (the page), CARD (things that group), CONTROL (things you press),
      WELL (recesses that hold a selection). You can tell them apart with the
      colour turned off, because they differ in fill AND edge AND elevation.

   3. CYAN MEANS ONE THING AGAIN. Ion cyan used to be the primary button, the
      selected tab, the applied filter and the current page — four meanings,
      one flat cyan slab, often on the same screen. Now a solid ion fill means
      "press this to do the thing" and nothing else. Selection is a raised
      neutral pill with an ion indicator bar under it, which is a different
      shape as well as a different colour.

   CONTRAST — every pair introduced here is measured, not estimated. Numbers
   are in the comment beside the rule. Nothing in this file drops a text pair
   below 4.5:1 or a state indicator below 3:1. The two boundary tiers are
   stated honestly where they are set (§1.4).
   ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ */


/* ══ 1. TOKENS ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ */

:root {

  /* ── 1.1 TYPE SCALE ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     Seven sizes on a ~1.18 ratio, and only THREE weights. Three, because the
     system stacks we ship on disagree about everything else: Segoe UI has
     Regular/Semibold/Bold and silently rounds 500 to Regular and 650 to
     Semibold, so a scale built on 400/500/600/650/700/800 renders as three
     weights on Windows anyway — it just renders as three UNPREDICTABLE ones.
     400 / 600 / 700 is the set every platform actually has.

     Tracking is size-specific because letters drift apart as they grow. The
     display sizes pull in, the caption sizes push out, uppercase pushes out a
     lot. (This continues the rule zs-brand.css established; it just now has
     sizes to attach to.) */
  --fs-hero:    24px;  --fw-hero:    700; --lh-hero:    1.12; --tr-hero:   -.028em;
  --fs-title:   19px;  --fw-title:   700; --lh-title:   1.20; --tr-t:      -.021em;
  --fs-lead:  16.5px;  --fw-lead:    700; --lh-lead:    1.24; --tr-l:      -.014em;
  --fs-row:   15.5px;  --fw-row:     600; --lh-row:     1.30; --tr-r:      -.006em;
  --fs-body:  14.5px;  --fw-body:    400; --lh-body:    1.45; --tr-b:       0;
  --fs-sub:     13px;  --fw-sub:     400; --lh-sub:     1.55; --tr-s:      .004em;
  --fs-eyebrow: 11.5px; --fw-eyebrow: 700; --lh-eyebrow: 1.20; --tr-e:      .13em;
  --fs-micro:  10.5px;

  /* ── 1.2 SPACING ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     Four values. The kit had sixty, which is why phone.html needs ten
     adjacency rules at the bottom of its <style> to patch vertical rhythm
     pair by pair. Everything below is one of these four or a row's
     min-height — nothing else. */
  --sp-1:  4px;   /* inside a chip; label→sub gap                */
  --sp-2: 10px;   /* between siblings in a row; control padding  */
  --sp-3: 16px;   /* card padding; the standard gutter           */
  --sp-4: 26px;   /* between one section and the next            */

  /* ── 1.3 RADIUS ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     Four values plus the pill. The three existing tokens are redefined here
     rather than replaced, so every one of their ~21 call sites moves at once
     and the 30 hardcoded radii scattered through the kit get restated below.
     Safe to set at :root: nothing in body.zs-oled or body.a11y-contrast
     touches radius, so no theme override is being outranked. */
  --r-chip:  8px;   /* tags, badges, the small stuff              */
  --r-ctl:  12px;   /* anything you press                         */
  --r-card: 18px;   /* anything that groups                       */
  --r-sheet:28px;   /* the one surface that comes up from the bottom */
  --r-pill: 999px;  /* a shape, not a scale step                  */

  /* ── 1.4 EDGES — two tiers, and the honest reason ────────────────────────
     WCAG 1.4.11 asks 3:1 for a boundary that IDENTIFIES a control or a state.
     It does not ask it of a decorative grouping edge, and a 3:1 grey outline
     around all eleven cards on the Overview screen reads as a wireframe, not
     a product. So:

       --line       containers  #43556c  2.58:1 vs --bg,  2.10:1 vs a card
                    (was #212b39 at 1.28:1 vs the card — invisible, which is
                    what the survey correctly called an accessibility defect
                    as well as an aesthetic one. This is a 64% improvement and
                    it is paired with a real fill delta and a lit top edge, so
                    the card no longer depends on its outline alone.)

       --zs2-edge   controls    #5c7089  3.88:1 vs --bg,  3.15:1 vs a card,
                    3.61:1 vs --panel, 3.44:1 vs an OLED card. Every button,
                    stepper, picker and toggle gets this one. It clears 3:1 on
                    every surface the app can put a control on, in every
                    theme — so the thing you can press is identifiable by its
                    boundary, which is exactly what 1.4.11 is about.

     High-contrast mode promotes BOTH past 3:1 — see §14. */
  --line:      #43556c;
  --zs2-edge:  #5c7089;

  /* ── 1.5 MATERIALS ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     Overlays, never hex fills. An overlay composites onto whatever --panel
     currently is, so body.zs-oled keeps its true black and still gets the
     same RELATIVE lift; a hardcoded #1c222a would have frozen OLED mode into
     looking like the default theme, which is how a theme quietly dies. */
  --zs2-card-a:  rgba(255,255,255,.055); /* card, top   → #1c222a on --panel */
  --zs2-card-b:  rgba(255,255,255,.022); /* card, bottom→ #141a23           */
  --zs2-ctl-a:   rgba(255,255,255,.120); /* control, top→ #2c3139           */
  --zs2-ctl-b:   rgba(255,255,255,.040); /* control, bot→ #191e27           */
  --zs2-sel:     rgba(255,255,255,.130); /* the selected segment pill        */
  --zs2-well:    rgba(0,0,0,.34);        /* a recess cut into a card         */
  --zs2-well-pg: rgba(255,255,255,.05);  /* a recess on the page ground —
                                            black on near-black is nothing,
                                            so on --bg the well goes UP       */
  --zs2-hair:    rgba(255,255,255,.075); /* divider INSIDE a card, 1.23:1 —
                                            deliberately quiet: the card edge
                                            does the grouping, the hairline
                                            only stops rows touching          */
  --zs2-sheen:   inset 0 1px 0 rgba(255,255,255,.075); /* light on a top edge */
  --zs2-groove:  inset 0 1px 2px rgba(0,0,0,.55);      /* light lost in a well */

  --zs2-ion-tint: rgba(111, 155, 255,.14);  /* icon containers, accent chips    */
  --zs2-ion-line: rgba(111, 155, 255,.55);  /* their edge — 3.72:1 vs a card    */
  --zs2-ion-dash: rgba(111, 155, 255,.45);  /* the "add something" dashes,
                                            3.02:1 vs --bg                   */
}


/* ══ 2. TYPE, APPLIED ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════
   Where hierarchy actually broke: the primary label of every settings row was
   14.5px/400 — the same weight as the paragraph in the note underneath it —
   while the uppercase header introducing it was 12px/700. The header was
   louder than its own content. Row labels now carry 15.5px/600 in --text and
   headers drop to 11.5px muted, so size and colour outrank weight and the
   order is right way up. */

.hero .nm {
  font-size: var(--fs-hero); font-weight: var(--fw-hero);
  line-height: var(--lh-hero); letter-spacing: var(--tr-hero);
}
.phead h2, .zs-gate h2 {
  font-size: var(--fs-title); font-weight: var(--fw-title);
  line-height: var(--lh-title); letter-spacing: var(--tr-t);
}
.appbar h1 { font-size: var(--fs-lead); letter-spacing: var(--tr-l); }

.cfg .cn .name, .sheet .shead h3, .aimph b, .smval, .smtitle {
  font-size: var(--fs-lead); font-weight: var(--fw-lead);
  line-height: var(--lh-lead); letter-spacing: var(--tr-l);
}

/* row titles — the most common line of text in the product */
.drow .t .lab, .item .t .lab {
  font-size: var(--fs-row); font-weight: var(--fw-row);
  line-height: var(--lh-row); letter-spacing: var(--tr-r); color: var(--text);
}
.flab, .acc > .ah, .salab {           /* .salab: the Smart Actions row label */
  font-size: 15px; font-weight: var(--fw-row);
  line-height: var(--lh-row); letter-spacing: var(--tr-r); color: var(--text);
}
.lrow .src { font-size: var(--fs-body); font-weight: var(--fw-row); letter-spacing: var(--tr-r); }
.card .cn  { font-size: 14px; font-weight: var(--fw-row); letter-spacing: var(--tr-r); }

/* secondary lines. One size (13px) for every explanatory string in the app —
   hints, notes, sub-labels, leads, card descriptions — because they are the
   same kind of sentence and were previously six sizes between 11.5 and 13.5. */
.drow .t .hint, .item .t .hint, .flab em, .note, .lead, .card .cd,
.hero .live, .idline .mt, .phead .psub, .smsub, .smcap, .pkr .pkn em,
.acc > .ab .lead, .ph {
  font-size: var(--fs-sub); font-weight: var(--fw-sub);
  line-height: var(--lh-sub); letter-spacing: var(--tr-s);
}

/* the eyebrow. 11.5px/700 uppercase at --muted: 6.45:1 on --bg, 5.24:1 on a
   card — both past AA — and unmistakably subordinate to a 15.5px/600 title. */
.grp, .sh, .pkh {
  font-size: var(--fs-eyebrow); font-weight: var(--fw-eyebrow);
  line-height: var(--lh-eyebrow); letter-spacing: var(--tr-e);
  text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--muted);
}

/* Numbers. Every live readout in this app changes in place many times a
   second; tabular figures stop the row reflowing under the finger. */
.stat b, .stp .sv, .flab .rv, .smval, .lrow .rt b, .dvb-meta b {
  font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; font-feature-settings: "tnum" 1;
}


/* ══ 3. CHROME — the two header bars ══════════════════════════════════════
   zs-brand.css already makes these glass and fades their lower boundary
   instead of ruling it. All that is missing is a lit top edge and enough
   room for 19px type. `body` prefix so the padding survives the runtime
   blocks. */

body .appbar, body .phead {
  box-shadow: var(--zs2-sheen);
  padding-left: var(--sp-3); padding-right: var(--sp-3);
}
body .phead { padding-bottom: var(--sp-2); gap: var(--sp-2); }
.phead .psub { color: var(--muted); margin-top: 1px; }

/* Back is the control every screen depends on, and it was a 14.5px word.
   44px minimum, ion, and its own optical margin so the glyph lines up with
   the 16px gutter rather than sitting 2px inside it. */
.pback, .appbar .cogbtn {
  min-height: 44px; min-width: 44px;
  font-size: var(--fs-body); font-weight: var(--fw-row);
  letter-spacing: var(--tr-r);
  border-radius: var(--r-ctl);
}


/* ══ 4. SECTION HEADERS — .grp and .sh ════════════════════════════════════
   These two were pixel-identical and yet collapsed differently against their
   siblings, which is the whole reason phone.html carries
   `.sh+.grp-card,.sh+.acc,…{margin-top:0}` at line 366. They are now one
   component with one rhythm: --sp-4 above, --sp-2 below, and the space under
   a header belongs to the header (§13 restates the adjacency rules so the
   card below never adds its own).

   The 2px ion tick is the only decoration in the file. It is identical on
   every header, carries no state, and is what makes eight consecutive
   sections read as eight sections rather than one long run. */
.grp, .sh, .pkh {
  display: flex; align-items: center; gap: var(--sp-2);
}
.grp  { padding: var(--sp-4) var(--sp-1) var(--sp-2); }
.sh   { margin:  var(--sp-4) 2px var(--sp-2); }
.pkh  { margin:  var(--sp-3) 2px var(--sp-2); }
.grp::before, .sh::before, .pkh::before {
  content: ""; flex: none; width: 2px; height: 11px;
  border-radius: 2px; background: var(--accent);
}
/* first thing on a screen should not be pushed down by a full section gap */
.dbody > .sh:first-child, .menu > .grp:first-child { margin-top: 4px; padding-top: 4px; }

/* the inline action a header can carry (Reorder, the live chip) */
.grp .gact {
  min-height: 44px; padding: 0 var(--sp-2); margin-right: -8px;
  font-size: var(--fs-sub); font-weight: var(--fw-row);
  letter-spacing: 0; text-transform: none; border-radius: var(--r-ctl);
}


/* ══ 5. SURFACES ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

   Four materials, and every box in the app is exactly one of them.

     CARD    groups things.        fill = card overlay, edge = --line,
                                   lit top edge, radius --r-card
     CONTROL you press it.         fill = control overlay, edge = --zs2-edge,
                                   lit top edge, radius --r-ctl, shadow el-1
     WELL    holds a selection.    fill = negative overlay, groove inset
     GROUND  the page.             --bg, untouched

   The card gradient runs light→dark top to bottom. That is not a flourish:
   it is the single cheapest way to say "this is a physical panel lit from
   above", and it is why the card no longer needs a 3:1 outline to be seen.
   Measured on the default theme, card top #1c222a is 1.23:1 against --bg and
   the lit edge above it is 1.26:1 against the card — small numbers doing a
   job that no single number could do alone. */

.hero, .cfg, .drow, .item, .grp-card, .acc, .vizbox, .tile, .card,
.mechcurve, .sastripbox, .sacat {          /* last two: beats a runtime block */
  background-image: linear-gradient(180deg, var(--zs2-card-a), var(--zs2-card-b));
  background-color: var(--panel);
  box-shadow: var(--zs2-sheen);
}

.grp-card, .acc, .vizbox {
  border: 1px solid var(--line);
  border-radius: var(--r-card);
  overflow: hidden;
}

/* ── 5.1 THE HERO ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   The most important element on the home screen used to differ from a plain
   config row by 1px of type and a box-shadow that composites to 1.02:1
   against the ground — mathematically invisible. It is now the only surface
   in the app carrying elevation AND the engraved reticle, so it is different
   in kind rather than in degree.

   CONSTRAINT 7. The left border is set INLINE by renderHome() to the console
   family colour. That means the `border` SHORTHAND is forbidden here — it
   would reset border-left-width to 1px and leave the inline colour painting a
   hairline where a 5px signal belongs. Longhands only, and border-left-color
   is never mentioned. Same rule on .cfg below. */
.hero {
  border-style: solid;
  border-width: 1px 1px 1px 5px;
  border-top-color: var(--line);
  border-right-color: var(--line);
  border-bottom-color: var(--line);
  border-radius: var(--r-card);
  box-shadow: var(--el-2), var(--zs2-sheen);
  overflow: hidden;
  position: relative;
}
.hero > * { position: relative; z-index: 1; }
.hero .top { padding: var(--sp-3) var(--sp-3) 14px; gap: 14px; }
.hero .art { border-radius: 14px; }

/* The reticle from the logo, engraved into the card's top-right corner and
   clipped by its radius. This is the only place the brand geometry appears
   below the app bar — the mark used to live on exactly one element in the
   whole product (.appbar h1 .brandmark) and the app read as anonymous
   because of it.

   ENGRAVED, NOT GLOWING, and that is a contrast decision rather than a taste
   one. A cyan watermark at 13% under the config name drops --muted on top of
   it to 3.97:1, which fails. Black at 32% RAISES it: --muted goes 5.24 → 5.89
   and --text 13.91 → 15.63. A darker watermark can never hurt legibility on a
   dark card, so it can be placed where a lighter one could not. */
.hero::after {
  content: ""; position: absolute; z-index: 0; pointer-events: none;
  right: -34px; top: -30px; width: 172px; height: 172px;
  --ret: rgba(0,0,0,.32);
  background-image:
    radial-gradient(circle at 50% 50%, transparent 0 43%, var(--ret) 43% 45.4%, transparent 45.9%),
    radial-gradient(circle at 50% 50%, transparent 0 13%, var(--ret) 13% 15%, transparent 15.5%),
    linear-gradient(var(--ret), var(--ret)),
    linear-gradient(var(--ret), var(--ret)),
    linear-gradient(var(--ret), var(--ret)),
    linear-gradient(var(--ret), var(--ret));
  background-size: 100% 100%, 100% 100%, 2px 17%, 2px 17%, 17% 2px, 17% 2px;
  background-position: 0 0, 0 0, 50% 6%, 50% 94%, 6% 50%, 94% 50%;
  background-repeat: no-repeat;
}

/* The board line. This carries the single most load-bearing fact in the
   product — "is my adapter actually driving the console" — and it was the
   smallest text on the page. It cannot be moved out of the hero without
   changing renderHome(), but it can stop being a footnote: its own strip,
   its own hairline, 13px, and a dot at 6.37:1. */
.hero .live {
  padding: 12px var(--sp-3);
  color: var(--muted);
  border-top: 1px solid var(--zs2-hair);
  background: rgba(255,255,255,.03);
}
.hero .live .dot { color: var(--green); font-size: 14px; }

/* "Edit this config" is a real action, so it is a real button rail. */
.hero .go {
  min-height: 54px; padding: 0 var(--sp-3);
  border-top: 1px solid var(--line);
  background-image: linear-gradient(180deg, rgba(255,255,255,.09), rgba(255,255,255,.05));
  background-color: var(--panel);
  color: var(--accent);                    /* 8.20:1 on that face */
  font-size: var(--fs-body); font-weight: var(--fw-row);
  letter-spacing: var(--tr-r);
}

/* ── 5.2 CONFIG ROWS ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   Longhand borders again — inline border-left-color. */
.cfg {
  border-style: solid;
  border-width: 1px 1px 1px 5px;
  border-top-color: var(--line);
  border-right-color: var(--line);
  border-bottom-color: var(--line);
  border-radius: var(--r-card);
  margin-top: var(--sp-2);
  padding: var(--sp-2) var(--sp-2) var(--sp-2) 14px;
  gap: 14px;
}
.cfg .cn .name { color: var(--text); }
.idline { margin-top: 3px; gap: 6px; }
.idline .mt { color: var(--muted); }        /* 5.24:1 on the card */

/* JS already writes aria-current="true" on the live row. Free state, no new
   class: the active config gets a brighter face and a defined edge instead
   of being the same rectangle with a tick bolted on. */
.cfg[aria-current="true"] {
  background-image: linear-gradient(180deg, rgba(255,255,255,.085), rgba(255,255,255,.035));
  border-top-color: var(--zs2-edge);
  border-right-color: var(--zs2-edge);
  border-bottom-color: var(--zs2-edge);
}
/* The tick AND the words — colour is never the only channel here, it never
   was, and the type change keeps it that way. #56d364 measures 8.31:1. */
.cstate {
  width: 58px; color: #56d364;
  font-size: var(--fs-micro); font-weight: var(--fw-eyebrow);
  letter-spacing: .06em; text-transform: uppercase; line-height: 1.25;
}
.cstate .tick { font-size: 17px; letter-spacing: 0; }

/* ── 5.3 NAVIGATION ROWS — .drow and .item ───────────────────────────────
   These were the same content model rendered two ways: a card on Home, a bare
   list line in the editor. The editor menu — the second screen every user
   reaches and the product's entire feature set — was five undecorated rows on
   the raw ground, 0 cards, 0 borders, 1 radius, 80% grey text. It is the most
   literal case of "this doesn't even look cool".

   One component now. .item gets its own horizontal margin because .menu pads
   `8px 0` and there is no wrapper element to add — the row supplies its own
   gutter, which needs no markup change at all. */
.drow, .item {
  border: 1px solid var(--line);
  border-radius: var(--r-card);
  min-height: 68px;
  gap: 14px;
  align-items: center;
}
.drow { padding: 14px var(--sp-3); margin-top: var(--sp-2); }
.item { padding: 14px var(--sp-3); margin: var(--sp-2) 14px; }
/* phone.html gives .item a bottom hairline that belonged to the flat list */
.item { border-bottom-color: var(--line); }
.drow:active, .item:active { background-color: var(--panel2); }

/* The icon container. It existed, it was 30px, it was --muted on --panel2,
   and it appeared on seven rows in the entire app. It is the cheapest
   scanning aid available — shape first, so it works with colour off. */
.drow .ico, .item .ico {
  width: 36px; height: 36px; border-radius: var(--r-ctl);
  background: rgba(255,255,255,.07);
  border: 1px solid var(--line);
  color: var(--text);      /* 11.37:1 on that tint — was --muted at 4.28:1,
                              which FAILED, and is the reason this line is a
                              fix and not a preference */
}
.drow .ico svg, .item .ico svg { width: 19px; height: 19px; }

/* THE FIVE-HUE EDITOR MENU IS ALREADY SHIPPING and this file must not touch
   it. phone.html's SECOND <style> block (line 587+) tints each editor row by
   section — .ico-overview ion, .ico-aim gold, .ico-bindings steel,
   .ico-smart orange, .ico-vision the brand gradient — via `.item .ico.ico-X`
   at (0,3,0), which out-specifies the (0,2,0) neutral container above. It
   survives untouched, which is correct: those hues already dodge the
   semantic set and the survey simply had not seen that block.

   What is left to fix is the FALLBACK. phone.html:225 still paints
   `.item.gated .ico` in rgba(88,166,255,…) — the PlayStation family blue as
   generic chrome — and today that is masked only because the one gated row
   happens to be the one .ico-vision repaints. Add a second gated section and
   the blue appears on an Xbox config.

   :not([class*="ico-"]) is the whole point of this selector: it retints ONLY
   an icon with no section tint of its own, so it can never overwrite the
   five hues. Ion glyph 6.70:1 on the tint, edge 3.72:1 against the card. */
.item.gated .ico:not([class*="ico-"]) {
  background: var(--zs2-ion-tint);
  border-color: var(--zs2-ion-line);
  color: var(--accent);
}
.chev, .drow .chev, .item .chev { color: var(--muted); font-size: 17px; }

/* ── 5.4 CARDS, TILES, PLACEHOLDERS ─────────────────────────────────────── */
.cards { gap: var(--sp-2); }
.card {
  border: 1px solid var(--line);
  border-radius: var(--r-card);
  padding: 14px var(--sp-2) var(--sp-3);
  display: flex; flex-direction: column; min-height: 118px;
}
.card .ci { color: var(--accent); }        /* 9.10:1 on the card */
.card .ci svg { width: 24px; height: 24px; }
.card .cn { margin-top: var(--sp-2); color: var(--text); }
.card .cd { color: var(--muted); margin-top: var(--sp-1); }
.card:active { background-color: var(--panel2); }

.tile {
  border: 1px solid var(--line);
  border-radius: var(--r-card);
  padding: var(--sp-3) var(--sp-2);
  position: relative;
}
/* Selected tile: 2px edge (a WIDTH change, readable with colour off), an ion
   wash, and a tick in the corner. Three channels, per constraint 6. */
.tile.on {
  border-width: 2px; border-color: var(--accent);
  padding: 15px 9px;                        /* hold the box the same size */
  background-image: linear-gradient(180deg, var(--zs2-ion-tint), rgba(111, 155, 255,.06));
}
.tile.on::after {
  content: "✓"; position: absolute; top: 6px; right: 8px;
  font-size: 12px; font-weight: 700; color: var(--accent);
}
.tile .tart { height: 48px; }

.ph, .newcfg, .addbtn {
  border-style: dashed; border-width: 1px; border-radius: var(--r-card);
}
.ph { border-color: var(--line); color: var(--muted); padding: var(--sp-3); background: none; }
.mechcurve { border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: var(--r-card); padding: var(--sp-3); }


/* ══ 6. SETTINGS ROWS INSIDE A CARD ═══════════════════════════════════════
   .fld is the workhorse — it is every toggle, slider, stepper and dropdown in
   the product. The row gets a real height so a 44px control has somewhere to
   live, and the divider gets quiet: the card's own edge does the grouping, so
   an interior rule only has to stop two rows touching. 1.23:1 is correct for
   that job and wrong for any other. */
.fld {
  padding: 13px var(--sp-3);
  min-height: 60px; gap: 14px;
  border-bottom: 1px solid var(--zs2-hair);
}
.fld.col { padding-top: 14px; padding-bottom: var(--sp-3); }
.flab em {
  display: block; color: var(--muted); margin-top: 3px; font-style: normal;
}
.flab .rv { color: var(--accent); font-weight: var(--fw-title); }  /* 9.10:1 */
.fld.tapper:active { background-color: rgba(255,255,255,.045); }
.grp-card + .grp-card { margin-top: var(--sp-2); }

/* list rows and stat rows share the card's interior rhythm */
.lrow {
  padding: 13px 0; gap: var(--sp-2); min-height: 54px;
  border-top: 1px solid var(--zs2-hair);
}
.grp-card > .stat, .grp-card > .lrow { padding-left: var(--sp-3); padding-right: var(--sp-3); }
.stat {
  padding: 12px 0; gap: var(--sp-2);
  border-top: 1px solid var(--zs2-hair);
  font-size: var(--fs-sub); color: var(--muted);
}
/* Every stat value used to be ion. Accent on 71 read-only numbers is accent
   meaning nothing — it is the same colour as the primary button. Values are
   --text/700 tabular (13.91:1); only a genuinely good state stays green. */
.stat b { color: var(--text); font-size: var(--fs-body); font-weight: var(--fw-title); }
.stat b.ok, .stat .ok { color: var(--green); }   /* 6.30:1 on the card */
.lrow .arrow { color: var(--muted); }
.lrow .rt { gap: var(--sp-2); }


/* ══ 7. BUTTONS ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
   Called out by name by the owner, so they get the most work in this file.

   A button is a KEY: a face lit from above, a defined edge at 3.88:1 against
   the page, one step of elevation, and 50px of height. Nothing here is
   decoration for its own sake — the lit top edge is what separates a button
   from a rectangle of slightly different grey, and the edge colour is what
   makes it identifiable without relying on the label. */

.btn {
  min-height: 50px;
  padding: 0 var(--sp-3);
  display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center;
  border: 1px solid var(--zs2-edge);
  border-radius: var(--r-ctl);
  background-image: linear-gradient(180deg, var(--zs2-ctl-a), var(--zs2-ctl-b));
  background-color: var(--panel);
  color: var(--text);                    /* 11.37:1 on the face */
  font-size: var(--fs-body); font-weight: var(--fw-row);
  letter-spacing: var(--tr-r);
  box-shadow: var(--el-1), var(--zs2-sheen);
  margin-top: var(--sp-2);
}

/* THE one solid ion surface in the app. It used to be shared with the
   selected tab, the applied filter and the current page; now a cyan slab
   means "press this and something happens" and nothing else.
   `body` prefix: this must beat zs-brand.css:252 AND the runtime blocks.
   Ink #041a2e measures 10.13:1 on the gradient's top and 8.43:1 on its
   bottom — AA at every point on the face, AAA over most of it. */
body .btn.primary {
  background-image: linear-gradient(180deg, rgba(255,255,255,.09), rgba(0,0,0,.07));
  background-color: var(--accent);
  border-color: var(--accent);
  color: var(--accent-ink);
  font-weight: var(--fw-title);
  box-shadow: 0 2px 14px var(--zs-ion-glow), inset 0 1px 0 rgba(255,255,255,.28);
}
/* Danger reads as danger by fill, edge AND label colour, so it survives
   greyscale: red label 5.33:1 on its wash, edge 3.14:1 against the page. */
.btn.danger {
  background-image: none;
  background-color: rgba(255,98,89,.13);
  border-color: rgba(255,98,89,.60);
  color: var(--red);
  box-shadow: none;
}
.btn.ghost {
  background-image: none; background-color: transparent;
  border-color: var(--line); box-shadow: none; color: var(--text);
}
/* Small still means tappable. 44px is the floor, not a suggestion — this used
   to render at ~35px, which is under the threshold on the one product whose
   whole purpose is lowering physical demand. */
.btn.sm {
  min-height: 44px; padding: 0 var(--sp-3);
  font-size: var(--fs-sub); font-weight: var(--fw-row);
  display: inline-flex; width: auto; margin-top: 0;
}

/* "add something" — one component, two call sites. These were the same
   button at radius 14 and radius 10. Ion dashes at 3.02:1 against the page,
   ion label at 11.20:1. */
.newcfg, .addbtn {
  min-height: 54px; padding: 0 var(--sp-3);
  border-color: var(--zs2-ion-dash);
  color: var(--accent);
  font-size: var(--fs-body); font-weight: var(--fw-row);
  margin-top: var(--sp-2);
  background: rgba(111, 155, 255,.045);
}

/* the small square icon button. 38px of visible key, 48px of hit area via the
   ::after expander phone.html already ships — the target is legal without the
   button having to look chunky. */
.ib {
  width: 38px; height: 38px;
  border: 1px solid var(--zs2-edge);
  border-radius: var(--r-ctl);
  background-image: linear-gradient(180deg, var(--zs2-ctl-a), var(--zs2-ctl-b));
  background-color: var(--panel);
  color: var(--text);
  font-size: 15px; font-weight: var(--fw-row);
  box-shadow: var(--el-1), var(--zs2-sheen);
}
.ib::after { inset: -5px; }              /* 48px hit target */

/* the "•••" that hides five settings behind it, and the curve preview */
.mechpill {
  min-height: 44px; padding: 0 22px;
  border: 1px solid var(--zs2-edge); border-radius: var(--r-pill);
  background-image: linear-gradient(180deg, var(--zs2-ctl-a), var(--zs2-ctl-b));
  background-color: var(--panel);
  color: var(--text); font-size: var(--fs-body);
  box-shadow: var(--el-1), var(--zs2-sheen);
}


/* ══ 8. SEGMENTED CONTROLS — .seg2, .subtabs, #edtabs ═════════════════════
   Four "pick one of N" controls with two resting backgrounds, two radii and
   four paddings, whose SELECTED state was byte-identical to the primary
   button. This is the change that gives cyan its meaning back.

   THE TRACK is a well: the group of options is one recessed object, so the
   choice reads as "one of these", not "four separate buttons that happen to
   be adjacent".

   THE SELECTED OPTION is a raised neutral pill with an ion indicator bar.
   Its state is carried on four independent channels, which is what
   constraint 6 asks for:
      · label colour  --text 11.04:1 on the pill  vs  --muted 5.94:1 on track
      · weight        700 vs 600
      · elevation     a lit, shadowed pill vs a flat track
      · the bar       ion at 7.22:1 on the pill, 10.32:1 on the track — this
                      is the ≥3:1 non-text indicator WCAG 1.4.11 wants, and
                      it is a shape, so greyscale keeps it. */

.seg2 {
  margin-top: var(--sp-2);
  gap: var(--sp-1); padding: var(--sp-1);
  background: var(--zs2-well);
  border: 1px solid var(--line);
  border-radius: calc(var(--r-ctl) + 4px);
  box-shadow: var(--zs2-groove);
}
.seg2 button, .subtabs button, #edtabs button {
  position: relative;
  min-height: 44px;
  padding: 12px 8px 14px;
  border: 1px solid transparent;
  background: none;
  border-radius: var(--r-ctl);
  color: var(--muted);
  font-size: var(--fs-sub); font-weight: var(--fw-row);
  letter-spacing: var(--tr-s);
}
/* beats zs-brand.css:252 and both runtime blocks */
body .seg2 button.on, body .subtabs button.on, body #edtabs button.on {
  background-image: none;
  background-color: var(--zs2-sel);
  border-color: rgba(255,255,255,.09);
  color: var(--text);
  font-weight: var(--fw-title);
  box-shadow: var(--el-1), var(--zs2-sheen);
}
body .seg2 button.on::after, body .subtabs button.on::after,
body #edtabs button.on::after {
  content: ""; position: absolute; left: 24%; right: 24%; bottom: 6px;
  height: 2px; border-radius: 2px; background: var(--accent);
}

/* The sticky sub-tab strip bleeds to the screen edge so it can occlude
   content scrolling under it; the TRACK therefore cannot be the element's own
   background, or it would bleed too. ::before draws the well inside the
   bleed. No markup change, and if a build ever drops the pseudo-element the
   strip degrades to exactly today's flat row. */
.subtabs {
  padding: var(--sp-2) 18px var(--sp-2);
  margin-bottom: var(--sp-2);
  gap: var(--sp-1);
}
.subtabs::before {
  content: ""; position: absolute; z-index: 0;
  left: 18px; right: 18px; top: 6px; bottom: 6px;
  border-radius: calc(var(--r-ctl) + 4px);
  background: var(--zs2-well-pg);
  border: 1px solid var(--line);
  box-shadow: var(--zs2-groove);
  pointer-events: none;
}
.subtabs button { z-index: 1; }

#edtabs {
  gap: var(--sp-1); padding: var(--sp-2) 14px;
  border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);
}


/* ══ 9. VALUE CONTROLS ════════════════════════════════════════════════════ */

/* ── 9.1 THE TOGGLE ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   Off and on differ by knob POSITION first, then track fill, then knob
   colour. Position is the channel that survives everything.
   knob #8b98a5 on the off track: 6.05:1 · ion knob on the on track: 4.77:1 ·
   the on track's ion border against the card: 9.10:1. */
.sw {
  width: 52px; height: 32px;
  background: var(--zs2-well);
  border: 1px solid var(--zs2-edge);
  box-shadow: var(--zs2-groove);
  position: relative;
}
/* NOT DONE HERE, on purpose. 52×32 is under the 44px floor, and the obvious
   fix — a ::before hit expander like the one .ib already carries — is not
   available: ::after is the knob (phone.html) and ::before is the ion bloom
   (zs-micro.css:47). Both pseudo-elements on .sw are spoken for, and quietly
   taking one would delete a shipped micro-interaction to fix a target the
   user can still hit by aiming at a 60px row. Growing the element and
   insetting the track re-homes the knob, which zs-micro.js then animates to
   the wrong place. This needs a wrapper element the renderer does not emit,
   so it is written down in the risks rather than bodged here. */
body .sw.on {
  background: rgba(111, 155, 255,.28);
  border-color: var(--accent);
}
body .sw.on::after { background: var(--accent); box-shadow: 0 0 10px var(--zs-ion-glow); }

/* ── 9.2 THE SLIDER ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
input[type=range].rng {
  height: 44px;
  background: linear-gradient(rgba(0,0,0,.34), rgba(0,0,0,.34)) 50% 50%/100% 8px no-repeat;
  border-radius: var(--r-pill);
}
input[type=range].rng::-webkit-slider-thumb {
  -webkit-appearance: none; width: 30px; height: 30px; border-radius: 50%;
  background: var(--accent);              /* 10.32:1 on the track */
  border: 4px solid var(--panel);
  box-shadow: 0 0 0 1px var(--accent), 0 2px 8px var(--zs-ion-glow);
}
input[type=range].rng::-moz-range-thumb {
  width: 26px; height: 26px; border-radius: 50%;
  background: var(--accent); border: 4px solid var(--panel);
  box-shadow: 0 0 0 1px var(--accent);
}

/* ── 9.3 THE STEPPER ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   A well with two keys cut into it. The value is 16px/700 tabular in --text
   because it is the answer to the question the row is asking, and it was
   13.5px/600 — smaller than the label above it. */
.stp {
  background: var(--zs2-well);
  border: 1px solid var(--zs2-edge);
  border-radius: var(--r-ctl);
  box-shadow: var(--zs2-groove);
  overflow: hidden; gap: 0;
}
.stp button {
  width: 46px; height: 46px;
  color: var(--accent);                    /* 10.32:1 on the well */
  font-size: 21px; font-weight: var(--fw-row);
}
.stp button:first-child { border-right: 1px solid var(--zs2-hair); }
.stp button:last-child  { border-left:  1px solid var(--zs2-hair); }
.stp .sv {
  min-width: 56px; padding: 0 var(--sp-1);
  font-size: 16px; font-weight: var(--fw-title); color: var(--text);
}
.stp .sv::after { font-size: var(--fs-sub); color: var(--muted); }
.stp input.svedit {
  border-radius: var(--r-chip); font-size: 16px; font-weight: var(--fw-title);
}

/* ── 9.4 SELECTS AND TEXT FIELDS ─────────────────────────────────────────
   Not on the brief's list, but they sit inside .fld on every screen and a
   restyled row with a stock select in it looks broken. */
select.sel, input.txt {
  min-height: 44px; padding: 0 var(--sp-2);
  border: 1px solid var(--zs2-edge);
  border-radius: var(--r-ctl);
  background-image: linear-gradient(180deg, var(--zs2-ctl-a), var(--zs2-ctl-b));
  background-color: var(--panel);
  color: var(--text);
  font-size: var(--fs-body); font-weight: var(--fw-row);
  box-shadow: var(--el-1), var(--zs2-sheen);
}
input.txt { padding: 0 14px; font-weight: var(--fw-body); }
.lrow .sel { min-height: 40px; font-size: var(--fs-sub); }

/* ── 9.5 PROGRESS ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   Styled but never emitted — it is the only home the brand gradient has, so
   it is kept correct rather than deleted. Ion against the well: 10.32:1. */
.prog {
  height: 10px; border-radius: var(--r-pill);
  background: var(--zs2-well);
  box-shadow: var(--zs2-groove);
  margin-top: var(--sp-2);
}
.prog > i { background: var(--zs-grad); border-radius: var(--r-pill); }

/* ── 9.6 SWATCHES ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   36px circles need a legal target too; ::after gives 46px without growing
   the dot, which has to stay small enough to sit six-across at 390px. */
.sc { position: relative; }
.sc::after { content: ""; position: absolute; inset: -5px; }


/* ══ 10. PICKERS — .pk, .pkr, .pbtn ═══════════════════════════════════════
   Two real bugs get fixed here, both found by measurement rather than by
   looking:

   1. `.pk` never declared a colour, so it inherited the UA button default:
      BLACK. It is masked today only because every child overrides it and the
      glyph SVGs carry their own fills. Any text placed directly in a .pk
      would render black-on-panel2 at 1.19:1.
   2. `.pk` and `.pbtn` both set font-family:inherit and forgot font-size, so
      they ran at 13.333px — the browser's default button size, and one of the
      ten font sizes counted on the Computer Vision screen.

   .pk and .pkr use the CARD face, not the control face, and that is
   deliberate: both carry --muted sub-labels, and --muted on the control face
   measures 4.28:1, which FAILS. On the card face it is 5.24:1, and inside a
   sheet 4.71:1. The material follows the text it has to carry. */
.pk {
  width: 58px; min-height: 58px;
  border: 1px solid var(--zs2-edge);
  border-radius: var(--r-ctl);
  background-image: linear-gradient(180deg, var(--zs2-card-a), var(--zs2-card-b));
  background-color: var(--panel);
  color: var(--text);                 /* fixes the inherited black */
  font-size: 12px;                    /* fixes the 13.333px UA default */
  box-shadow: var(--zs2-sheen);
  padding: var(--sp-1) 2px; gap: 3px;
  position: relative;
}
.pk .pklab { font-size: var(--fs-micro); color: var(--muted); }
.pk.wide { width: 84px; }
.pk.cur {
  border-width: 2px; border-color: var(--accent);
  padding: 3px 1px;                   /* hold the box the same size */
  background-image: linear-gradient(180deg, var(--zs2-ion-tint), rgba(111, 155, 255,.05));
}
/* The tick goes on the LABEL's ::before, not the cell's ::after, because
   .pk::after is zs-micro.css:155 — the lock ring it flashes when a button is
   chosen. A `.pk.cur::after` here would out-specify that and silently kill
   the animation. Where a cell is glyph-only and has no .pklab the tick is
   simply absent and the 2px border carries the state, which is the graceful
   degradation this whole file is written to. */
.pk.cur .pklab::before { content: "✓ "; color: var(--accent); font-weight: 700; }

.pkr {
  min-height: 60px; padding: var(--sp-2) 14px; gap: 14px;
  border: 1px solid var(--zs2-edge);
  border-radius: var(--r-ctl);
  background-image: linear-gradient(180deg, var(--zs2-card-a), var(--zs2-card-b));
  background-color: var(--panel);
  color: var(--text);
  font-size: var(--fs-body); font-weight: var(--fw-row);
  box-shadow: var(--zs2-sheen);
  margin-top: var(--sp-2);
}
/* the leading glyph gets the same container the menu rows use, so a picker
   row and a settings row look like relatives */
.pkr .pki {
  width: 36px; height: 36px; border-radius: var(--r-ctl);
  background: var(--zs2-ion-tint);
  border: 1px solid var(--zs2-ion-line);
  color: var(--accent);
}
.pkr .pki svg { width: 20px; height: 20px; }
.pkr .pkn em { color: var(--muted); margin-top: 2px; }
.pkr.cur { border-width: 2px; border-color: var(--accent); padding: 9px 13px; }

/* the picker TRIGGER — shows the current choice, opens the sheet */
.pbtn {
  min-height: 44px; padding: 0 var(--sp-2); gap: var(--sp-2);
  border: 1px solid var(--zs2-edge);
  border-radius: var(--r-ctl);
  background-image: linear-gradient(180deg, var(--zs2-ctl-a), var(--zs2-ctl-b));
  background-color: var(--panel);
  color: var(--text);
  font-size: var(--fs-sub);            /* fixes the 13.333px UA default */
  box-shadow: var(--el-1), var(--zs2-sheen);
}
.pbtn .plab { font-size: var(--fs-sub); font-weight: var(--fw-row); }
.pbtn .pchev { color: var(--text); font-size: 13px; }  /* --muted here is
                                                          4.28:1 and fails */
.pkgrid { gap: var(--sp-2); }


/* ══ 11. CHIPS — .tag, .badge, .pill ══════════════════════════════════════
   Six chip types existed at 11 / 11 / 12 / 12 / 12.5 / 11.5px and radii
   6 / 6 / 999 / 999 / 999. Two shapes now, and the shape is the meaning:

     rounded rect (--r-chip)  METADATA — a fact about the thing
     pill (--r-pill)          STATUS   — something that is true right now

   All of them are outline chips by default. A filled chip at --muted measured
   4.28:1 and failed; --muted on the card behind it is 5.24:1 and passes, so
   the fill goes and the border stays. */
.tag, .badge {
  min-height: 22px; padding: 2px var(--sp-2);
  border: 1px solid var(--line);
  border-radius: var(--r-chip);
  background: none;
  color: var(--muted);
  font-size: var(--fs-eyebrow); font-weight: var(--fw-row);
  letter-spacing: var(--tr-s);
  display: inline-flex; align-items: center;
}
.pill {
  min-height: 22px; padding: 2px 11px;
  border-radius: var(--r-pill);
  border: 1px solid var(--zs2-ion-line);
  background: var(--zs2-ion-tint);
  color: var(--accent);                  /* 6.70:1 on its own wash */
  font-size: var(--fs-eyebrow); font-weight: var(--fw-row);
}
/* .tag.accent used the PlayStation blue as a generic "interesting" colour.
   Retinted: ion label 6.70:1 on the wash, edge 3.72:1 against the card. */
.tag.accent {
  background: var(--zs2-ion-tint);
  border-color: var(--zs2-ion-line);
  color: var(--accent);
}
/* green here is semantic (Xbox / live), and it is never alone — the label
   says the word. #56d364 6.35:1 on its wash, edge 4.82:1 against it. */
.tag.on {
  background: rgba(63,185,80,.16);
  border-color: var(--green);
  color: #56d364;
}


/* ══ 12. NOTES, ACCORDIONS, OVERLAYS ══════════════════════════════════════ */

/* ── 12.1 THE NOTE — the app's most-emitted component ────────────────────
   Nineteen per screen on Computer Vision, eighteen on Aim, and each one was a
   12.5px grey paragraph inside a BLUE box — rgba(88,166,255,.08), the
   PlayStation family colour, used as the generic information wash on Xbox
   screens too. Ninety per cent of the Smart Actions screen is grey text, and
   this component is why.

   A note stops being a box. It is a margin note: a 2px rule and an indent,
   which is what a note has looked like in print for four hundred years. The
   consequence is the point — when nineteen of them stop drawing boxes, the
   three things on the screen that ARE boxes finally read as boxes.

   --muted on the page ground: 6.45:1. Rule at 2.58:1. */
.note {
  background: none;
  border: none;
  border-left: 2px solid var(--line);
  border-radius: 0;
  padding: 2px 0 2px 14px;
  margin-top: var(--sp-3);
  color: var(--muted);
}
.note b { color: var(--text); font-weight: var(--fw-row); }

/* A warning keeps its box, and now it is the ONLY note with one — which is
   the entire reason warnings were invisible before: every note looked like a
   callout, so a callout meant nothing. warnNotes() already prefixes "⚠", so
   the signal is glyph + box + colour, never colour alone.
   --muted on the amber wash 5.14:1 · amber rule vs the wash 7.08:1. */
.note.warn {
  background: rgba(224,163,58,.10);
  border: 1px solid rgba(224,163,58,.35);
  border-left: 3px solid var(--amber);
  border-radius: var(--r-ctl);
  padding: 12px 14px;
}
.lead { color: var(--muted); margin: 0 0 var(--sp-2); }

/* ── 12.2 THE ACCORDION ─────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
.acc > .ah {
  min-height: 56px; padding: 0 var(--sp-3); gap: var(--sp-2);
}
.acc.open > .ah { border-bottom: 1px solid var(--zs2-hair); }
.acc > .ah .aicon { color: var(--muted); }
.acc.open > .ah .aicon { color: var(--accent); }   /* 9.10:1 — and the chevron
                                                      rotation carries the same
                                                      state without colour */
.acc > .ah .chev { color: var(--muted); font-size: 17px; }
.acc > .ab { padding: 14px var(--sp-3) var(--sp-3); }
.acc + .acc, .acc { margin-top: var(--sp-2); }
.acc > .ah:active { background-color: rgba(255,255,255,.045); }

/* ── 12.3 THE SHEET ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   The biggest radius in the app, on the one surface that comes up from the
   bottom edge. zs-brand.css owns its glass and its grabber; this is geometry
   and type only. */
.sheet { border-radius: var(--r-sheet) var(--r-sheet) 0 0; }
.sheet .shead { padding: 20px var(--sp-3) var(--sp-2); }
.sheet .shead h3 { color: var(--text); }
.sheet .shead .sclose {
  width: 42px; height: 42px;
  border: 1px solid var(--zs2-edge);
  background-image: linear-gradient(180deg, var(--zs2-ctl-a), var(--zs2-ctl-b));
  background-color: var(--panel);
  color: var(--text); font-size: 16px;
}
.sheet .sbody { padding: 2px var(--sp-3) 22px; }

/* ── 12.4 THE TOAST ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   A small floating chip, so it takes the pill shape rather than a card
   radius — it is not a panel and should not look like one.
   --text on the toast 13.78:1 · ion action 9.01:1. */
.toast {
  border-radius: var(--r-pill);
  min-height: 48px; padding: var(--sp-2) 20px;
  font-size: var(--fs-body); font-weight: var(--fw-row);
  letter-spacing: var(--tr-r);
  gap: var(--sp-3);
}
.toast button { font-size: var(--fs-body); font-weight: var(--fw-title); padding: var(--sp-2); }


/* ══ 13. VERTICAL RHYTHM ══════════════════════════════════════════════════
   phone.html patches spacing with ten adjacency rules because there is no
   owned stack. Those rules are (0,2,0) and load before us, so they win over a
   plain .grp-card{margin-top}. Rather than fight them with !important, the
   same adjacencies are restated here at the same specificity in the right
   order: everything gets the section gap, and then anything immediately after
   a header gets nothing, because the header already spent that space in §4.

   ORDER IS LOAD-BEARING in this block. The reset must come second. */
.note + .grp-card, .vizbox + .grp-card, .acc + .grp-card,
.btn + .grp-card, label + .grp-card,
.grp-card + .note, .grp-card + .btn, .grp-card + .acc,
.note + .btn, .btn + .btn {
  margin-top: var(--sp-3);
}
.sh + .grp-card, .sh + .acc, .sh + .vizbox, .sh + .note, .sh + .btn,
.sh + .cards, .sh + .seg2, .sh + .lead, .grp + .drow, .grp + .cfg,
.grp + .hero, .grp + .item {
  margin-top: 0;
}
.vizbox { margin-top: var(--sp-3); padding: var(--sp-3); }
.dbody { padding-left: 18px; padding-right: 18px; }


/* ══ 14. ACCESSIBILITY ════════════════════════════════════════════════════
   Five signals, each honoured on its own terms. None of them is treated as a
   downgrade — high contrast gets a BETTER edge, not a cheaper one. */

/* ── 14.1 MORE CONTRAST ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
   Both edge tiers are promoted past 3:1 and the decorative sheens come off,
   because a gradient is exactly the thing that blurs a boundary for someone
   who asked for boundaries.
     --line     #74829a  4.71:1 vs --panel, 4.12:1 vs a card
     --zs2-edge #8c9bb2  6.98:1 vs --bg,    5.68:1 vs a card
   These are set on :root here and on body.a11y-contrast below; the body rule
   has the higher specificity, so the app's own switch always wins over the
   OS's, which is the behaviour a user who set both would expect. */
@media (prefers-contrast: more) {
  :root { --line: #74829a; --zs2-edge: #8c9bb2; --zs2-hair: rgba(255,255,255,.20); }
  .hero, .cfg, .drow, .item, .grp-card, .acc, .vizbox, .tile, .card,
  .btn, .pk, .pkr, .pbtn, .ib, .mechcurve { background-image: none; box-shadow: none; }
  .hero::after { display: none; }               /* no watermark under text */
  .tile.on, .pk.cur, .pkr.cur { border-width: 3px; }
  body .seg2 button.on, body .subtabs button.on, body #edtabs button.on {
    border-color: var(--text);
  }
  .note { border-left-width: 3px; }
}
body.a11y-contrast {
  --line: #74829a; --zs2-edge: #8c9bb2; --zs2-hair: rgba(255,255,255,.20);
}
body.a11y-contrast .hero::after { display: none; }
body.a11y-contrast .hero, body.a11y-contrast .cfg, body.a11y-contrast .drow,
body.a11y-contrast .item, body.a11y-contrast .grp-card, body.a11y-contrast .acc,
body.a11y-contrast .vizbox, body.a11y-contrast .tile, body.a11y-contrast .card,
body.a11y-contrast .btn, body.a11y-contrast .pk, body.a11y-contrast .pkr,
body.a11y-contrast .pbtn, body.a11y-contrast .ib {
  background-image: none;
}
/* --muted becomes #bcc7d3 in this mode: 9.34:1 on a card, 11.49:1 on the
   ground, and 7.63:1 even on the bright control face — so the one pairing
   that fails at default contrast passes comfortably here. */

/* ── 14.2 REDUCED TRANSPARENCY ──────────────────────────────────────────
   Every overlay in this file composites onto an OPAQUE parent, so nothing
   here is see-through in the sense the setting means. What does go is the
   two places a translucent surface stacks on another one: the well drawn
   over the sticky sub-tab strip, and the tinted press states. */
@media (prefers-reduced-transparency: reduce) {
  .subtabs::before { background: var(--panel); }
  .seg2 { background: var(--bg); }
  .stp, .sw, input[type=range].rng, .prog { box-shadow: none; }
  .fld.tapper:active, .acc > .ah:active { background-color: var(--panel2); }
  .newcfg, .addbtn { background: none; }
}

/* ── 14.3 REDUCED MOTION ────────────────────────────────────────────────
   This file adds no animation and no transition, so there is nothing to turn
   off — zs-brand.css §7 and body.a11y-motion already cover the press springs.
   The only motion-adjacent thing added is the ::after tick on a selected tile
   or picker cell, and a tick that simply exists cannot trigger anything. The
   rule below is here so the intent is on the record rather than implied. */
@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
  .tile.on::after, .pk.cur::after, .hero::after { transition: none; animation: none; }
}

/* ── 14.4 BIGGER TOUCH TARGETS (the app's own switch) ───────────────────
   phone.html grows .ib, .sw and .stp button under body.a11y-big at (0,2,0),
   which already outranks this file's (0,1,0) sizes — nothing to redo. What
   DOES need restating is the segmented padding, because the indicator bar
   sits 6px off the bottom and a11y-big's `padding:15px 8px` would let a
   descender reach it. Equal specificity, later source. */
body.a11y-big .seg2 button, body.a11y-big .subtabs button,
body.a11y-big #edtabs button { padding: 16px 8px 19px; }
body.a11y-big .btn { min-height: 56px; }
body.a11y-big .btn.sm { min-height: 48px; }
body.a11y-big .fld { min-height: 68px; }
body.a11y-big .drow, body.a11y-big .item { min-height: 76px; }

/* ── 14.5 OLED ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   --panel drops to #0a0d11 and --bg to true black, and because every surface
   above is an OVERLAY rather than a hex fill, the whole system moves with it:
   a card lands at #171a1e (1.20:1 against black, vs 1.23:1 against --bg in
   the default theme — the same relationship, not a broken one).
   The edges keep their jobs: --line 2.75:1 against pure black, --zs2-edge
   4.13:1. The one thing worth nudging is the card lift, because black is
   less forgiving than near-black. */
body.zs-oled {
  --zs2-card-a: rgba(255,255,255,.075);
  --zs2-card-b: rgba(255,255,255,.030);
}

/* ── 14.6 FOCUS ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   Untouched from zs-brand.css — 2px ion at 11.20:1 against the ground, with a
   5px wash halo. Restated only where a new radius would let the ring clip. */
.item:focus-visible, .drow:focus-visible, .cfg:focus-visible,
.hero .go:focus-visible { border-radius: var(--r-card); }
.btn:focus-visible, .seg2 button:focus-visible, .subtabs button:focus-visible,
.pbtn:focus-visible, .ib:focus-visible { border-radius: var(--r-ctl); }


/* ══ 15. TWO RUNTIME-INJECTED BLOCKS, RECONCILED ══════════════════════════
   smart-actions.js and phone-sections.js each inject a stylesheet at runtime
   that re-solves problems this kit already solves. They land in <head> after
   us, so these three rules carry a `body` prefix to win on specificity.

   Only the constraint violations are corrected — the rest of those blocks is
   left alone, because rewriting a stylesheet this file cannot see the whole
   of is how a design system breaks a screen it was never shown. */

/* .satile.sel used rgba(88,166,255,.35) — the PlayStation family colour — as
   a generic "this one is selected" glow, on every screen including Xbox
   configs. Same fix as .item.gated: ion, plus a width change so the state is
   not colour alone. */
body .satile.sel {
  border-color: var(--accent);
  border-width: 2px;
  box-shadow: 0 0 0 2px var(--zs-ion-glow);
}
/* .sapreview is a card wearing the PlayStation wash for the same reason. */
body .sapreview {
  background: rgba(255,255,255,.045);
  border-color: var(--line);
  border-radius: var(--r-card);
}
/* the family/filter chips and tiles inherit the kit's two shapes */
body .safil button, body .sapill { border-radius: var(--r-pill); min-height: 40px; }
body .satile { border-radius: var(--r-ctl); }


/* ══ 16. THE AIM PAGER ════════════════════════════════════════════════════
   The best-designed screen in the app and the only place a card layout
   already existed — so this is type only. Its own (0,2,0)/(0,3,0) overrides
   in phone.html keep the centred-stack layout; these match that specificity
   so the sizes move with the rest of the scale and the layout does not. */
.aimpage .fld .flab { font-size: 15px; font-weight: var(--fw-row); }
.aimpage .fld .flab em { font-size: var(--fs-sub); line-height: var(--lh-sub); }
.aimpage .fld .stp .sv { font-size: 19px; font-weight: var(--fw-title); }
/* phone.html:407 shrinks the stepper keys to 44×40 inside an aim page, which
   is under the 44px floor on the axis a thumb actually misses. Restated at
   matching specificity — measured in the browser, not assumed. */
.aimpage .fld .stp button { width: 46px; height: 46px; font-size: 21px; }
.aimph { border-radius: var(--r-card); padding: 14px var(--sp-3); }
.aimph em { font-size: var(--fs-sub); }
.aimdots i { background: var(--line); }        /* 2.58:1 — was 1.28:1 */
.aimdots i.on { background: var(--accent); }   /* 11.20:1 against the ground */
.smcar .smval {
  min-width: 150px; min-height: 48px;
  border: 1px solid var(--zs2-edge); border-radius: var(--r-ctl);
  background-image: linear-gradient(180deg, var(--zs2-ctl-a), var(--zs2-ctl-b));
  background-color: var(--panel);
  box-shadow: var(--el-1), var(--zs2-sheen);
}
.smcar .arr { color: var(--accent); font-size: 26px; }
.smblk { padding: 14px var(--sp-3) 2px; }
.mechcurve span { color: var(--muted); font-size: var(--fs-sub); }
